Do you want to jump in here?

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:30.680
- Yeah. Thanks.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:34.010
Yeah, Abigail's advice
is absolutely spot on,

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.610
but we've had
Articulation training.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.940
Articulation is a
consultancy group.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.525
We've been working with
Posit to help the speakers

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:46.970
get ready for Posit Conf,

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:49.430
and the advice
has been absolutely fantastic.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.840
I've fundamentally changed what
my slide deck and presentation

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.075
looks like from
before I started.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.975
What they got us
to think about is,

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:04.880
what is the hook? So what
is the thing that you're going

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.870
to try and draw people's
attention in because we're all

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.630
dreadfully distracted
by everything

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:13.340
in the room?

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:19.400
And so you want to hook people
in, and then what are, if you

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.020
like, three key

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:26.080
items that you're going to
want them to remember later.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.435
And then once you've
got those things,

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.180
you may or may not have a
call to action at the end

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:38.576
of the presentation. But

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:43.100
once what is your hook,
so what is the key theme,

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.290
and then what are the three

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.805
things that you want people
to remember afterwards, only then

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.820
flesh out each of those three
and then think about what is

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.640
the visuals, or the

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:00.895
cues, code,

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:06.160
images, whatever. You want
that support those three elements.

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:08.320
I,

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:10.370
think many people,

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.370
I was used to opening up
PowerPoint and jumping in and

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.830
just, waaah, loads and loads
of text. And it's really,

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.100
really hard to then
edit that down.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:23.290
Whereas if you step away
from PowerPoint, or Keynote,

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:25.390
or whatever it is you're
using or even Quarto,

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.930
and just think about what
are those things that I've just

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:29.965
articulated then--

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.180
And actually I used a mind map

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:35.430
tool,

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:39.730
which allows me to think about
what are those things and then

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.210
explode each of those and go,

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:44.530
what are the topics or what
things must remember to say about those,

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.650
but then work back from that.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.650
And once I've thought that
through, then I open up

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:52.660
PowerPoint. And it, as I say,

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:53.965
it's turned around
what I've done.

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:57.520
- Yeah, that's a
great point, Mike.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.350
I remember that
from last year too.

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:02.120
And I just took a
bunch of sticky notes,

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.330
and I remember sitting down
at my kitchen table and just

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:07.840
writing like as many ideas as
I had about the topic and just

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.160
putting it all over the
table and then trying to organize

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:15.520
those thoughts a bit into
little groups and then thinking

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:19.375
from then, what would
the presentation look like too.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:23.100
Hey, Amanda. I see your hand
raised. You want to jump in?

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:24.680
- Yeah.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.150
Full disclosure, I used
to be an educator. So

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.970
I have a lot of experience with

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.380
keeping people engaged

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.790
especially when there's a lot
going on. The conference is

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:40.740
busy. Some people
are there virtually,

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:44.070
and you want them to listen
to what you're saying and focused

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:48.060
on you. So definitely as few
words as possible on the slide.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:51.810
And something that are now
hybrid world has given us the

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:55.470
opportunity to do is put a
code or a link on your first

00:07:55.470 --> 00:08:00.010
slide to your slides and your
notes. So you can tell people,

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:04.680
hey, this is available to
you. So you don't need to be

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:06.870
frantically writing notes
if you don't need to.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:09.390
Everything's in there.
Why don't we just talk,

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.190
and then keep it
very, very simple.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.910
And that's going to help people
focus on what you're saying

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:16.830
more when they have
that relief like,

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.545
I don't need to write everything
down. This is available to me.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:20.930
- Great points.

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:25.120
I will try and catch
up in the chat too,

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.985
but I see Honest you had
your hand raised as well.

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:29.690
- Yes.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:31.610
This is

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:35.520
earth shattering, but
it's easier to remember,

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:38.100
and it's more interesting
to listen to when your

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:39.285
presentation has a story.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:43.260
So just to remind people when
they think about preparing

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:46.455
their presentation if they can
share a story because that will

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:50.670
capture attention and
people will enjoy it more.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:55.380
- Yeah, I'm really enjoying this
image you put in the chat

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:59.820
too, Jake, about examples of a
bad slide and a good slide.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:04.200
Maybe there's some way I can
share that with the YouTube recording too.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:05.620
JAKE: Yeah, absolutely.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:08.220
I just have a really hard
time when someone puts just a wall

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:12.210
of text up. It's a little
hard to follow. So, yeah,

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.280
I think bolding is
really helpful too,

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.095
so not just change a
color but make it bold.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:18.210
- The other thing was

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.180
visuals have

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:25.250
a limited number per slide.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.760
It's easy to overwhelm the
audience with a billion things

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:31.695
that they need to look
at or on some text.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.635
And so the things that I've

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:39.970
done for my slide deck at
the minute is have one big visual

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.995
and one small amount
of text in big font.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:45.600
Repeat

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:48.805
and be consistent in
how those things look.

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:52.150
And consistent visual

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:53.820
design

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:56.020
also just helps people

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:59.890
just go, it's OK. I don't
need to fret that this is a

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:03.340
different animation style, or

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:06.670
things are different styles.

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:09.330
It's just you can concentrate
on the actual words.

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:09.390
-

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:13.530
I found that the big images
for a slide really helped me

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:15.330
remember a presentation too.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:16.610
Even

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:20.310
my own talk last year
about community building,

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:23.280
I was thinking like, what
were my four main points?

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:25.530
And in my head I
pictured those four,

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:29.490
the big images on a slide. And
then I'm like, OK, I can remember.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:34.330
Let's see.

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:37.960
Any other thoughts in the chat
that somebody wants to jump in

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:39.220
and share here too.

