00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:05.260
I feel the anxiety
we all have about

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:09.200
multiple talks happening
at one time, trying

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:10.990
to add them all.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:13.615
One topic,

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:17.290
or one follow up question
on the topic of presentations I

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:18.850
was curious for you all,

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:22.270
when you're putting
together slides for internal

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:23.500
presentations,

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:27.740
do you ever send that
information out ahead of time

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:30.590
with a summary
before a meeting,

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:33.200
or is it usually in the
meeting that's the first time

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:38.130
people see it? I never know
sometimes sending something out

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:38.880
ahead of a meeting,

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:42.000
are people actually even
going to go through it,

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.785
or do you assume
that they will?

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:45.975
MARCOS: I would say, yes,

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:48.630
ahead of time. But usually,

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:52.650
I feel like the kind of
storytelling streamline talk

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:55.050
you would do at this conference
is very different from the kind

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.530
of slide decks I tend to send out
at work or that get sent around at work.

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:01.140
The ones that work,
they're information dense,

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:03.030
they have a lot of numbers, a
lot of tables, a lot of charts.

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.730
You're intending to spend
time with it, and read it,

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:06.810
and stare at the numbers,

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:10.215
and understand the report out or
the results of the test or whatever,

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:14.450
which is very different than
this talk like the Posit talk

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:16.400
where they're encouraging us
to have minimal stuff on the

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:19.460
screen, and people are supposed
to listen to our words.

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:23.870
So I think it's super important
for information dense decks that are half

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:28.970
a document to be read and
half to be talked through as opposed

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:33.050
to this sort of conference
where I think the decks will be

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:34.610
considerably less
information dense.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:38.510
- I guess,

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:41.420
I'm trying to also figure
out what's the difference then in

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:45.050
presenting a story to people
in a conference and getting them

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:48.200
to understand what you're
saying versus getting your

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:49.770
colleagues to understand

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:53.390
the key story
behind the data too.

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:53.900
MARCOS: Yeah,

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:57.260
I've been thinking about that
because I wonder how useful.

00:12:57.260 --> 00:13:00.020
We have an internal
conference here for analysts,

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.780
and I was telling some
folks maybe something like

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:03.530
Articulation Inc.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:07.110
Coming to us before the
next internal conference would be good.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:08.840
But that's because
for that conference,

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:12.670
it's a lot of people reporting
out of their area of expertise.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:13.550
So it's like people,

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:16.670
or teams talking to teams
that don't usually work in them,

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:20.940
so a giant blast of
numbers and data is

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.450
out of context and you need--

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:25.040
a storytelling
would be helpful.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:25.540
Whereas

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.180
if it's your own team
and you're just kind of like,

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:31.080
people already know,

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.270
heard what you've been talking
about for a couple of months,

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:34.410
and then you're
just showing, well,

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:35.880
here's what happened
with the A/B test,

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.440
