stuff beforehand.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.220
I feel like I'm dominating
the last 5 minutes,

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:45.930
so I will let someone else.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.490
- No, that's it's helpful.

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:51.150
Daniel, I thought
you had some chats,

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:54.120
or you had something in
the chat here about presentations

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:56.160
for leadership. Would
you want to jump in?

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:56.660
DANIEL: Yeah.

00:13:57.630 --> 00:13:59.220
And I apologize for
the background noise.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:02.960
I like to keep my
slides very light.

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:05.980
I like to use lots of images,
use a bunch of [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:09.850
color and whatnot. But when
you're meeting with leadership,

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:13.550
you have a very short amount of
time to convey a bunch of information.

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:17.620
So what I usually do is take
all of the images from my slide

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:19.990
deck and put them
into a sales slick.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:23.230
It's usually four or five pages
but it has all of the detail

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:25.020
that you're going to
cover in the presentation

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:29.170
for both. So when
you send out both,

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:32.770
now they can see the slide deck
but they can also see all of

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:34.450
the detail that you're
going to be providing,

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:38.140
all of the backup and all
of the narrative prior to the

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:40.480
meeting. So then they
come in prepared,

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.340
and they can ask really
good questions. And

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:44.740
for me, it's been
pretty successful.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:46.330
- That's a great point.

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:48.530
