00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:45.085
- I was on mute. Yeah,
the job I'm in currently

00:24:45.850 --> 00:24:48.850
when I first joined, there
was pretty clear like, hey,

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:53.455
already when you join, here's the
plan for 30, 60, 90 days.

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:57.955
We have a project for
you coming in. We expect this

00:24:59.440 --> 00:25:01.105
amount of progress,
and we'll check in.

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:05.020
And that'll help you get both
get your feet wet but then also

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:07.135
provide this clear direction

00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:12.210
rather than just ambiguous--

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:16.750
sit-in on meetings and pitch
in where you can is very--

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:21.670
you'll get up to speed at
some point with that method,

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:25.150
but it's just a lot quicker
I feel with that planned ahead of

00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:26.395
time from the hiring manager.

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:28.450
- Absolutely.

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:31.850
Amanda, sorry, I missed
your hand was raised before.

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:33.705
- Oh, yeah.

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:37.390
I was just going to say a lot
of times in my experience,

00:25:37.390 --> 00:25:38.340
when you're onboarding,

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:41.880
people seem to still be they're
worried you're not up to date

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:45.430
on the skills. But they've
just vetted you on your skills.

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:50.280
And so the best thing you
can do for somebody is the specifics.

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:55.140
Like, this is how we connect with
our server, and this is how you--

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:57.900
the specifics to your area.

00:25:57.900 --> 00:26:01.750
We have this platform here
that requires this funky code.

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:03.970
Those are the things
that frustrate people the most.

00:26:03.970 --> 00:26:04.860
And so those are--

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:06.300
if you're onboarding somebody,

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.535
those are the little details
that they really need.

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:12.390
And then the other thing that
we're doing in my current

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:17.170
position is, we have markdowns,
notebooks whatever for

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:19.020
all of our meetings.

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:22.710
All the way from here's how
you access the spreadsheet,

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:25.950
we put it in every
single one. So somebody's onboarding has

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:30.990
the opportunity to just go
through this stack of notebooks

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:34.630
or whatever and see how people
have done things in the past.

00:26:35.590 --> 00:26:38.710
And that a lot of times
those little details are actually in there.

00:26:40.140 --> 00:26:40.380
-

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:43.950
Something I want to get
better about when I'm helping onboard

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:48.270
somebody or just doing an
intro call with them is just

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:50.580
reminding myself that
they're being thrown so much

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:52.710
information right now,

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:56.680
and they're not going to
retain every single piece of information.

00:26:56.680 --> 00:27:01.860
So if I could be the person
to actually tell them a story

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:05.960
about a customer or something
that they're able to remember

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:08.100
rather than

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:13.050
just throwing more facts at them
and giving them more time to

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:15.940
come ask about the facts,

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:19.275
but to at least understand the
culture by the story that I'm telling.

