00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:32.310
but I completely
agree with the idea.

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:34.440
When we onboard new
data scientists,

00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:36.495
I assume that they

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:41.370
know Python, know pandas, they know
how to be a data scientist,

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:44.070
but they don't know all the
quirks of where our data is

00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:47.340
hidden, how the weird
parquet files that we use,

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:49.620
not weird but the unique
parquet files that we use to

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:50.520
store our data and

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:54.010
how it's organized
by date, tables.

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:57.270
So we have some very
formal SQL training that introduces people

00:27:57.270 --> 00:27:59.010
to our enterprise,

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:02.400
SQL type tables.

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:06.000
But then I have written some
Jupyter Notebooks that are very much--

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:06.270
they don't--

00:28:06.270 --> 00:28:08.190
they're very lead by the nose.
They just basically show,

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:10.140
and so something they can
refer back to later like,

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:12.660
how do I load this kind
of data? Oh here, it is.

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:13.620
I did it in this notebook.

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:15.510
And so it leads them
through a step by step like,

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:17.310
loading some data,
making a toy model,

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:20.820
using some of the quirky tools
that we built internally.

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:23.440
And I do think that is
the most important thing,

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:25.620
at least for new
data people is. Yeah,

00:28:25.620 --> 00:28:27.540
we know they can
understand trends,

00:28:27.540 --> 00:28:28.860
and they can do data analysis,

00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:31.680
but what are the quirks of our
system and how can we best help

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:34.395
a new hire understand
those quirks.

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:38.590
- Absolutely. Adrian, I
saw your hand was raised.

00:28:39.610 --> 00:28:40.370
- Hi. Yeah,

00:28:41.620 --> 00:28:46.406
so a couple of comments that it
