00:53:38.680 --> 00:53:39.940
what's happening?

00:53:39.940 --> 00:53:42.580
And then at least you've kind
of commiserated together,

00:53:42.580 --> 00:53:43.900
and you're not alone anymore.

00:53:43.900 --> 00:53:47.230
So the mentoring has never
really been one to one. But,

00:53:47.230 --> 00:53:48.490
as you say, if
you find friends,

00:53:48.490 --> 00:53:50.180
then you can go up
to people and say,

00:53:50.180 --> 00:53:51.160
can you help me with this,

00:53:51.160 --> 00:53:54.190
particularly if it kind of
strays into more career focused

00:53:54.190 --> 00:53:56.620
as opposed to problem focused.

00:53:56.620 --> 00:53:59.560
You can then trust
people because you've tested their

00:53:59.560 --> 00:54:02.290
technical knowledge as much as
anything and that they're there

00:54:02.290 --> 00:54:04.930
for you when you're really
struggling with stuff.

00:54:04.930 --> 00:54:06.550
So, yeah, go about
it a different way.

00:54:06.550 --> 00:54:09.130
I think maybe just be
part of a community.

00:54:09.130 --> 00:54:10.870
Just listening
sometimes helps well.

00:54:12.280 --> 00:54:14.665
We call them lurkers
I suppose in Slack,

00:54:15.370 --> 00:54:16.750
and that's fine. That's great.

00:54:16.750 --> 00:54:19.180
People are really taking a
lot away from the Slack groups.

00:54:20.230 --> 00:54:21.260
- I love that point.

00:54:21.260 --> 00:54:25.300
So it's just helpful sometimes to
know somebody else can't do it either.

00:54:29.290 --> 00:54:32.920
I wanted to circle back
for a second to George's question

00:54:32.920 --> 00:54:34.070
before because Alan,

00:54:34.070 --> 00:54:37.210
I see you shared some
other thoughts in the chat.

00:54:37.210 --> 00:54:41.050
Is it OK if I pull
you in here to share that?

00:54:42.340 --> 00:54:44.875
- Yeah, I think
my thoughts were

00:54:46.030 --> 00:54:47.190
regardless of the rollout

00:54:48.190 --> 00:54:50.800
method the way you make
that data available.

00:54:50.800 --> 00:54:53.785
I think being sort
of a facilitator or enabler

00:54:54.550 --> 00:54:58.120
as long as it takes is
really important so that you set up

00:54:58.120 --> 00:55:01.030
really clear expectations about
what is this data good for,

00:55:01.030 --> 00:55:04.120
what does it support well versus
what does it not support well.

00:55:04.910 --> 00:55:07.330
And that way you
can be riding along,

00:55:07.330 --> 00:55:11.110
helping people think about in
your case compliance issues or

00:55:11.110 --> 00:55:13.360
those kinds of
things in particular.

00:55:13.360 --> 00:55:14.710
And it's a whole lot easier,

00:55:15.440 --> 00:55:17.980
and we found this over
the course of trying to course

00:55:17.980 --> 00:55:21.670
correct some big long
multiyear projects,

00:55:21.670 --> 00:55:24.740
it's a whole lot easier to
do that at the beginning than later.

00:55:24.740 --> 00:55:28.920
So it's really exciting to roll
stuff out and also it's really,

00:55:28.920 --> 00:55:30.500
really helpful
and important to--

00:55:30.500 --> 00:55:33.890
everybody look around the table
and you go, OK, wait. Let's

00:55:34.670 --> 00:55:36.890
do this slowly. Let's do
this really carefully.

00:55:37.430 --> 00:55:41.090
But it's a real balance of
feeling success on the part of

00:55:41.090 --> 00:55:46.490
your team and your mission
and setting up your stakeholders

00:55:46.490 --> 00:55:49.415
for longer term success
and stability and stuff.

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:52.785
- Thanks so much, Alan.

